The popup is in place. Because it offers a discount and leads into weekly promos, a few POPIA rules shaped how it works, so here is what I built and why.

## How it works

1. A visitor enters their email to get **10% off their first order**. The code shows on screen straight away and is also emailed once. Delivering a code someone asked for is a service message, not marketing.
2. **Weekly promos are a separate, unticked opt-in** in the same popup. These visitors aren't customers yet, so the s69 existing-customer exception doesn't apply and you need their prior opt-in, in wording substantially similar to Form 4 (Reg 6). **The discount doesn't depend on ticking the box.** POPIA consent must be voluntary (s1), and making the 10% conditional on accepting marketing would undermine that.
3. If they tick it, they get a **double opt-in** confirmation email. Only confirmed addresses get promos.
4. If they don't tick it, they get only the code. **Don't email them asking for consent later.** You may ask a non-customer only once (s69(2)), and this popup is that ask. After a first purchase, the existing-customer exception may let you market your own similar products to them, provided every message offers an opt-out.

## What I added

### Popup component (`src/components/DiscountPopup.tsx`)

