  async function submit(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const f = new FormData(e.currentTarget);
    const { error } = await supabase.auth.signUp({
      email: String(f.get('email')),
      password: String(f.get('password')),
      options: {
        captchaToken,                                   // verified server-side by Supabase Auth
        emailRedirectTo: `${location.origin}/welcome`,  // account inactive until email confirmed
        data: { display_name: String(f.get('display_name')) },
      },
    });
    if (!error) {
      // Record marketing consent as an event (who, wording version, when), not just a boolean.
      // Do this server-side after confirmation, e.g. in a trigger or edge function.
      setSent(true);
    }
  }

  if (sent) return <p>Check your inbox to confirm your email and finish signing up.</p>;

  return (
    <form onSubmit={submit}>
      <label htmlFor="display_name">Display name</label>
      <input id="display_name" name="display_name" required maxLength={40} />
      <p className="text-xs">Shown next to recipes you share. Doesn't need to be your real name.</p>

      <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
      <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required autoComplete="email" />
      <p className="text-xs">We'll send a link to confirm it's you. Used to sign in and for account messages.</p>

      <label htmlFor="password">Password</label>
      <input id="password" name="password" type="password" required minLength={10} autoComplete="new-password" />

      {/* Only include if the app is 18+. Neutral wording, no hint of how to bypass. */}
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox" checked={adult} onChange={e => setAdult(e.target.checked)} required />
        I confirm I am 18 or older.
      </label>

      {/* s69: separate, optional, unticked */}
      <label>
        <input type="checkbox" checked={marketing} onChange={e => setMarketing(e.target.checked)} />
        Email me recipe round-ups and app news. I can unsubscribe at any time.
      </label>

      <Turnstile siteKey={import.meta.env.VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY} onSuccess={setCaptchaToken} />

      {/* s18 notice at collection */}
      <p className="text-xs">
        We use these details to create and secure your account. See our{' '}
        <a href="/privacy">Privacy Notice</a> for how long we keep them and your rights,
        including the right to complain to the Information Regulator.
      </p>

      <button type="submit" disabled={!captchaToken}>Create account</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Config to switch on:
- **Supabase Dashboard → Authentication → Providers → Email:** enable "Confirm email".
- **Authentication → Attack Protection:** enable CAPTCHA (Turnstile or hCaptcha) and put the *secret* key there, not in the frontend. The site key in `VITE_*` is public by design and fine.
- **Rate limits:** keep Auth rate limits on (defaults are reasonable).
- **`profiles` table:** RLS enabled, owner-scoped policies, and a purpose comment (`display_name`: shown publicly on recipes; basis: contract; retention: until account deletion).

What I did, briefly: removed the ID field, kept sign-up to email + password + display name, put email confirmation and bot protection in its place, kept marketing opt-in separate and unticked, and added the collection notice next to the form.

## If you genuinely need verified identity later

If something changes (for example, you start paying out to recipe creators and need FICA/tax details), that's the point to collect an ID number, for that group only, with the treatment POPIA expects: Luhn + date validation, field-level encryption or tokenisation, masked display (last 3 digits), named-role access with read logging, never as a key or URL parameter, and a clear "why we ask" notice. Selfie-based KYC against Home Affairs is biometric processing and needs a separate, explicit decision (and ideally an attorney's view) before you build it.

