I'd push back on the "big Accept, small grey settings link" layout. Below is a design that still looks good and converts well, without the legal risk.

## Why the asymmetric layout is a problem

- **The consent wouldn't hold up.** POPIA defines consent as a *voluntary, specific and informed* expression of will (s1), and the burden of proof is on you (s11(2)(a)). A banner where "yes" is one big click and "no" is hidden behind a faint link is a textbook dark pattern. It's hard to argue the person chose freely.
- **Marketing cookies are where the risk is.** POPIA has no cookie-specific section, but practitioners read the Information Regulator's Direct Marketing Guidance Note (Dec 2024) as bringing marketing and advertising trackers (Meta Pixel, Google Ads, TikTok, LinkedIn Insight) into the electronic-marketing frame. Treat those as **opt-in only**. Treat analytics as needing at least notice plus a real choice.
- **The pixels themselves add exposure.** They usually send data to the US, which is a cross-border transfer (s72) and has to be disclosed (s18(1)(g)). Pre-consent firing of pixels is a High finding in any audit.
- **The banner has to block, not just inform.** Nothing non-essential can load until the person has chosen. That's where most banners actually fail.

## The design: three equal choices

```
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Cookies on [Site]                                                         │
│                                                                           │
│ We use necessary cookies to make this site work. With your permission     │
│ we'd also like to use analytics cookies to improve the site, and          │
│ marketing cookies (Meta, Google Ads) to show you relevant ads elsewhere.  │
│ Some providers are outside South Africa. Change your choice any time      │
│ under "Cookie settings" in the footer.  Read our Cookie Notice            │
│                                                                           │
│   [  Reject all  ]     [  Choose  ]     [  Accept all  ]                  │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Rules for the visual design**
- **Equal weight for Reject and Accept:** same size, same font weight, same contrast. Marketing can use the brand colour, but then both buttons use it (or both are outlined). "Choose" can be secondary, but it has to be a real button, not grey link text.
- **Placement:** a bottom bar or corner card that doesn't cover the content. No cookie wall, and no "by continuing to browse you accept".
- **Defaults:** nothing pre-ticked. In the "Choose" panel, Analytics and Marketing toggles start **off**.
- **Copy:** plain language. Name the main providers and say that some are offshore.
- **Accessibility:** `role="dialog"`, labelled heading, keyboard reachable, visible focus states, and WCAG AA contrast on *every* button. The grey link marketing proposed would likely fail contrast on its own.
- **Changing your mind:** a permanent "Cookie settings" link in the footer reopens the panel. Withdrawing must be as easy as consenting.

**"Choose" panel**

| Category | Toggle | Description shown |
|---|---|---|
| Necessary | Always on (disabled) | Keeps the site secure and working (session, security, load balancing). |
| Analytics | Off | Helps us see which pages are used, so we can improve them. Provider: [Plausible / GA4]. |
| Marketing | Off | Lets [Meta, Google Ads] show you our ads on other sites and measure them. Data goes to the US. |

Buttons in the panel: **Save choices**, **Reject all** and **Accept all**, all the same size.

## What to give marketing instead

- **Better data from the people who do accept.** Consented users give clean attribution, and Google Consent Mode v2 models conversions for those who decline.
- **Measurement that doesn't depend on the banner.** Cookieless, privacy-friendly analytics (Plausible, Fathom, or self-hosted Umami) still needs disclosure, but gives you traffic numbers from nearly everyone.
- **A better yes-rate through copy, not layout.** Brand-styled buttons, friendly wording, and a clear "why" lift acceptance without the risk.

## Build it

If you're on React, Next.js, Vite or Lovable, **install the consent banner and gate from `registry/`** (see `registry/README.md`) rather than hand-rolling one. It already follows these rules. If you need it standalone, here's the core:

```html
<!-- 1. Before ANY Google tag: deny by default -->
<script>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag(){ dataLayer.push(arguments); }
  gtag('consent', 'default', {
    ad_storage: 'denied', ad_user_data: 'denied',
    ad_personalization: 'denied', analytics_storage: 'denied'
  });
</script>

<!-- 2. Banner -->
<div id="cc" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="cc-title" aria-describedby="cc-desc" hidden>
  <h2 id="cc-title">Cookies on [Site]</h2>
  <p id="cc-desc">
    We use necessary cookies to make this site work. With your permission we'd also like to use
    analytics cookies to improve the site, and marketing cookies (Meta, Google Ads) to show you
    relevant ads elsewhere. Some providers are outside South Africa. Change your choice any time
    under "Cookie settings" in the footer. <a href="/cookies">Read our Cookie Notice</a>
  </p>
  <div class="cc-actions">
    <button type="button" class="cc-btn" data-cc="reject">Reject all</button>
    <button type="button" class="cc-btn" data-cc="choose">Choose</button>
    <button type="button" class="cc-btn" data-cc="accept">Accept all</button>
  </div>
  <form id="cc-panel" hidden>
    <label><input type="checkbox" checked disabled> Necessary (always on)</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" name="analytics"> Analytics</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" name="marketing"> Marketing</label>
    <div class="cc-actions">
      <button type="button" class="cc-btn" data-cc="reject">Reject all</button>
      <button type="submit" class="cc-btn">Save choices</button>
      <button type="button" class="cc-btn" data-cc="accept">Accept all</button>
    </div>
  </form>
</div>

<style>
  #cc { position: fixed; inset: auto 1rem 1rem 1rem; max-width: 44rem; margin: auto;
        background: #fff; color: #111; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: .75rem;
        box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.15); z-index: 1000; }
  .cc-actions { display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 1rem; }
  /* Same style for every choice: equal prominence */
  .cc-btn { flex: 1 1 10rem; padding: .75rem 1rem; font: 600 1rem/1 inherit;
            border-radius: .5rem; border: 2px solid var(--brand, #0b5);
            background: var(--brand, #0b5); color: #fff; cursor: pointer; }
  .cc-btn:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #000; outline-offset: 2px; }
</style>

<script>
  const CONSENT_VERSION = 'cookies-v1';  // bump when categories or providers change, to re-ask
  const KEY = 'cc_consent';
  const el = document.getElementById('cc');
  const panel = document.getElementById('cc-panel');

  function read() {
    try {
      const c = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY));
      return c && c.version === CONSENT_VERSION ? c : null;
    } catch { return null; }
  }
  function load(src) { const s = document.createElement('script'); s.src = src; s.async = true; document.head.appendChild(s); }

  function apply(c) {
    gtag('consent', 'update', {
      analytics_storage: c.analytics ? 'granted' : 'denied',
      ad_storage: c.marketing ? 'granted' : 'denied',
      ad_user_data: c.marketing ? 'granted' : 'denied',
      ad_personalization: c.marketing ? 'granted' : 'denied'
    });
    if (c.analytics) load('https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXX'); // or Plausible
    if (c.marketing) { /* load Meta Pixel, LinkedIn Insight, TikTok here, and ONLY here */ }
  }

  function save(analytics, marketing) {
    const c = { version: CONSENT_VERSION, analytics, marketing, ts: new Date().toISOString() };
    try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(c)); } catch {}
    // Evidence (s11(2)(a)): log the choice server-side with the wording version.
    // Use an anonymous consent ID, not PI.
    navigator.sendBeacon?.('/api/consent', JSON.stringify(c));
    el.hidden = true;
    if (analytics || marketing) apply(c);
    else if (window.__ccHadConsent) location.reload(); // withdrawal: stop scripts that are already running
  }

  el.addEventListener('click', e => {
    const a = e.target.dataset?.cc;
    if (a === 'accept') save(true, true);
    if (a === 'reject') save(false, false);
    if (a === 'choose') { panel.hidden = false; panel.querySelector('[name=analytics]').focus(); }
  });
  panel.addEventListener('submit', e => {
    e.preventDefault();
    save(panel.analytics.checked, panel.marketing.checked);
  });

  // Footer "Cookie settings" link: <a href="#" data-cookie-settings>Cookie settings</a>
  document.addEventListener('click', e => {
    if (e.target.matches('[data-cookie-settings]')) {
      e.preventDefault(); el.hidden = false; panel.hidden = false;
    }
  });

  const existing = read();
  window.__ccHadConsent = !!(existing && (existing.analytics || existing.marketing));
  if (existing) apply(existing); else el.hidden = false;
</script>
```

**Also do these:**
- **Remove hard-coded tags.** Take any Meta Pixel, GA or TikTok snippets out of your theme, `index.html` or GTM "All Pages" triggers. They must only load through `apply()`. In GTM, use consent-based triggers.
- **Server-side tracking counts too.** Meta CAPI or server-side GTM have to respect the same choice. Hashing an email isn't anonymisation.
- **Cookie Notice (s18):** list each cookie or tool, its purpose, the provider, where it processes data (US for Meta and Google), how long it lasts, and how to change your choice. `scripts/popia_pack.py` can draft one from a short questionnaire.
- **ROPA:** record Meta, Google and any other tag vendors as operators, with their s72 basis.
- **Test it.** Run `python3 scripts/site_check.py https://yoursite.co.za --out site-check.md` in a fresh browser profile. On first load, before any click, there should be **no** `_fbp`, `_ga`, `_gcl_*` or TikTok cookies, and no requests to `facebook.com/tr` or `google-analytics.com`. Then check that Reject keeps it that way.

If marketing still wants the asymmetric version after this, put the decision to your Information Officer in writing.

